hi this is going to be a video about the  push to hub api for tensorflow and keras  so to get started we'll open up our  notebook  and the first thing you'll need to do is  log into your hugging face account for  example with the notebook login function  um so to use that  you simply call the function the pop-up  will emerge you i'll enter your username  and password which i'm going to pull out  of my password manager here  and you log in  the next two cells are just getting  everything ready for training so we're  just going to load a data set we're  going to tokenize that data set and then  we're going to load our model and  compile it with a standard atom  optimizer so i'm just going to run all  of those  we'll wait a few seconds  and everything should be ready for  training  okay  so  now we're ready to train uh i'm going to  show you the two ways you can push your  model to the hub so the first is with  the push to hub callback  so a callback in keras  is a function that's called regularly  during training you can set it to be  called after a certain number of steps  or every epoch or even just once at the  end of training  um  so a lot of callbacks in keras for  example control learning rate decaying  on plateau  and things like that  so this callback by default will save  your model to the hub once every epoch  and that's really helpful because  especially if your training run is very  long because it means you can resume  from that save so you get this automatic  cloud saving of your model and you can  even run inference uh with the the  checkpoints of your model that have been  uploaded by this call this callback  and that means you can you know add do  run some test inputs and i actually see  how your model works at various stages  during training which is a really nice  feature  so  we're going to add the push-to-hope  callback and it takes just a few  arguments so the first argument is the  temporary directory that um files are  going to be saved to before they're  uploaded to the hub the second argument  is the tokenizer and the third argument  is here is the keyword argument the hub  model id so that's the name it's going  to be saved under on the hugging face  hub  you can also upload to an organization  account  um just by adding the organization name  before the repository name with a slash  like this so you probably don't have  permissions to upload to the hugging  face organization if you do please  follow a bug and let us know extremely  urgently  but if you do have access to your own  organization then you can use that same  approach to upload models to their  account instead of to your own personal  um  set of models  so  once you've made your callback you  simply um add it to the callbacks list  when you're calling model.fit and  and everything is uploaded for you from  there and there's nothing else to worry  about  the second way to upload a model though  is to call model.push2hub  so this is more of like a once-off  method  it's not called regularly during  training you can just call this manually  whenever you want to upload a model to  the hub  so we recommend  running this after the end of training  um just to make sure that you like you  have a commit message to guarantee that  this was the final version of the model  that was at the end of training and it  just makes sure that you know you're  you're working with the definitive end  of training model and not accidentally  using a checkpoint from somewhere along  the way  um  so i'm going to run both of these cells  and then i'm going to cut the video here  just because training is going to take a  couple of minutes so i'll skip forward  to the end of that when the models have  all been uploaded and i'm going to show  you how you can  access the models in the hub and the  other things you can do with them from  there  okay we're back  our model was uploaded and both by the  push to hope callback and also by our  call to model.push2hub after training  so everything's looking good so now if  we drop over to  my profile and hugging face and you can  get there just by clicking the profile  button in the drop down we can see that  the burt fine-tuned cola model is here  as updated three minutes ago  so it will always be at the top of your  list because they're sorted by how  recently they were updated  and we can start querying our model  immediately  so  uh  the  data set we were training on is the  blue cola data set and cola is an  acronym standing for the corpus of  linguistic acceptability uh so what that  means is the model is being trained to  decide if a sentence is sort of  grammatically or linguistically okay or  if there's a problem with it  uh for example  we could say  this is a legitimate sentence and  hopefully it realizes that this is in  fact a legitimate sentence  um  so it might take a couple of seconds for  the model to load when you call it for  the first time  so i might cut a couple of seconds out  of this video here  okay we're back  so the model loaded and we got an output  um but there's an obvious problem here  so these labels  aren't really telling us  you know what categories the model has  actually assigned to this input sentence  so if we want to fix that we want to  make sure the model config  has the correct names for each of the  label classes and then we want to upload  that config  so we can do that down here to get the  label names um we can get that from the  the data set we loaded um from the the  features attribute it has and then we  can create the two  dictionaries id to label and label to id  and just assign them to the model config  and then we can just push our updated  config and that will overwrite the  existing config in the  hub repo so that's just been done  so now if we go back here i'm going to  use a slightly different sentence  because sentences are and the outputs  for sentences are sometimes cached and  so  if we want to generate new results i'll  just use something slightly different so  let's try an incorrect sentence  so this is not valid english grammar and  hopefully the model will decide it's  going to reload it here so i might cut a  couple of seconds again  and then we'll see what the model has to  say  okay  so the model its confidence isn't very  good because of course we you know we  didn't really optimize our hyper  parameters at all and but it has decided  that this sentence is more likely to be  unacceptable than acceptable  um presumably if we tried a bit harder  with training we could get a much lower  validation loss and therefore the  model's predictions would be more  precise but let's try our original  sentence again  of course because of the caching issue  we're seeing that the original answers  are  unchanged so let's try a different valid  sentence  so let's try this is a valid english  sentence  and we see that now the model correctly  decides that it has a very high  probability of being acceptable and a  very low probability of being  unacceptable  so  you can use this inference api even with  the checkpoints that are uploaded during  training so it can be very interesting  to see how  the model's predictions for sample  inputs change  with each ebock of training  also the model we've uploaded is going  to be accessible to you and if it's  shared publicly to anyone else and so if  you want to load that model um all you  or anyone else needs to do  is just to you can either load it in a  pipeline or you can  just load it with for example tf auto  model for sequence classification  and then instead for the name you would  just simply pass  the path to the repo you want to upload  or to download excuse me so if we if a  if i want to use this model again if i  want to load it from the hub i just run  that one line of code  the model will be downloaded  and  with any luck it'll be ready to  fine-tune under different data set make  predictions with or do anything else you  want to do  so  that was a quick overview of how after  your training or during your training  you can upload models to the hub you can  checkpoint there you can resume training  from there and you can  get inference results from the models  you've uploaded  so thank you and i hope to see you in a  future video
 

